{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Only, just as the gazelle and the deer are eaten, so you shall eat it</b> — this is as he [Moses] will explain: the impure and the pure together. For since in the wilderness He commanded that they not eat cattle and sheep except as sacrificial offerings of well-being, and He warned them concerning these — \"the person who eats flesh from the sacrifice of well-being that is HaShem's while his impurity is upon him shall be cut off\" — He says here that they may eat according to all the desire of the soul in profane meat, and that they may eat it with the impure person in a single bowl, just as they eat the gazelle and the deer in the wilderness, which is not offered upon the altar. And the meaning of <b>like the gazelle and like the deer</b> is not that one may eat the forbidden fat of profane animals just as one eats the fat of the gazelle and the deer, for he would mention only the manner of eating — in the sense of \"as you eat the gazelle and the deer, so you shall eat the flesh that you slaughter in any place\" — and he did not say \"whatever is eaten of the gazelle and the deer may be eaten of it.\" Therefore he explains in every place (Deuteronomy 15:22): \"the impure and the pure together shall eat it, like the gazelle and like the deer,\" and he did not say in any place <b>you shall eat it like the gazelle and like the deer</b> alone, so that the forbidden fat too would be included. And the meaning of \"only\" (אך) is a warning, because at first it was forbidden to eat anything except the well-being offerings slaughtered at the Tabernacle, and now that He permitted slaughtering and eating \"within your gates\" and \"according to all the desire of your soul,\" it would be understood that one may eat even profane meat and also consecrated offerings as before, which one would offer there at a bamah. Therefore He said and warned: <b>only, just as the gazelle and the deer are eaten, so you shall eat it</b> — that you shall not offer from it any forbidden fat or blood, and you shall not treat them in the manner of consecrated offerings, and you need not warn the impure from them at all. And it was necessary to mention (Deuteronomy 12:16), <b>only the blood you shall not eat</b>, for many reasons: first, because at the beginning He stated in the prohibition of blood (Leviticus 17:11), \"and I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls,\" and from this it would be understood that blood which does not make atonement in the case of a non-sacrificial animal would not be forbidden; therefore it was necessary to forbid it explicitly. But concerning forbidden fat He did not say likewise at the time of its prohibition, and not everything that is offered on the altar is forbidden, nor is everything that is not offered permitted, as I have written in its place (Leviticus 3:9). Furthermore, since He commanded here that we slaughter and warned that we eat in the manner of the gazelle and the deer, it would have been understood that we must cover the blood just as He commanded us concerning the gazelle and the deer (Leviticus 17:13); in any case it was necessary to permit that we pour the blood like water without covering. And this is the meaning of <b>upon the earth</b> (עַל הָאָרֶץ) and not <b>in the earth</b> (בָּאָרֶץ), which would mean covering it with dust. And since it was necessary to mention the permissibility of the blood, He preceded this by explaining its prohibition. Furthermore, Scripture was concerned lest they err regarding blood, because in the wilderness, at the time when all their cattle and sheep were well-being offerings, the meat was forbidden until the blood was sprinkled upon the altar — for such is the law concerning offerings — and behold, in them the blood is a condition for permitting the eating of the meat. And He commanded concerning the gazelle and the deer, since they are not offered, that one must cover their blood with dust, and likewise concerning the bird, since the bird is not brought as a well-being offering. If so, one might have thought that for cattle and sheep of profane slaughter one would be required to cover it, or to eat it together with the meat through niḥurah (slaughtering by severing the trachea and esophagus from within, without draining) so that it would not be visible upon the face of the field — for it would have been fitting that it be permitted to eat it on its own, given that one pours it upon the earth — \"that they may no longer sacrifice their sacrifices to the goat-demons after whom they go astray.\" Therefore He said that one slaughters the cattle and the sheep and does not eat the blood together with the meat by niḥurah or as a limb from a living animal, and pours the blood upon the earth and pays it no heed, but does not eat it — neither on its own nor together with the meat. And for this same reason it was necessary to mention this once more in the case of a firstborn with a blemish (Deuteronomy 15:23), because the blood was originally designated for the altar, and now it was necessary to say that when it has a blemish, one shall not treat its blood with sanctity nor permit it, but shall pour it like water and not eat it.\n\nAnd what seems right in my eyes regarding the reason for this matter: at first, when they were in the wilderness — \"the howling of the jackal and the dancing of the goat-demons\" — and they were all Egyptians who had gone forth, accustomed to them, He forbade exposing blood upon the face of the field and slaughtering in general, except before the Tabernacle of HaShem, in order to distance them from that sin. But when they came into the land and it became necessary to permit meat of desire because of the distance from the central sanctuary, He was not concerned if the blood of cattle and sheep was spilled in their homes upon the earth. But regarding the blood of wild animals and birds that are hunted in the field and in the forest — where the practice is to slaughter them there and bring them already slaughtered to the house — He left the commandment as it had been, to cover their blood with dust, so that they would not offer it to the goat-demons.\n\nAnd the midrash of our Sages (Sifrei 42) on <b>Only be strong not to eat the blood</b> and <b>you shall not eat it, that it may go well with you</b> (Deuteronomy 12:25) is a fine and fitting midrash for the language of Scripture: they were powerfully drawn after it, therefore all these warnings were necessary. And all the more so was it necessary to mention its prohibition even though He did not mention the prohibition of forbidden fat. And indeed, this statement of our Sages suffices for the reason behind the multiplicity of warnings with which Scripture warns concerning it. But the language that He said — <b>Only be strong not to eat the blood</b> — does not sit well with me in all this, for what strength and valor is there in abstaining from blood? And it would be more fitting to express its stringency by saying <b>Take heed to yourself not to eat the blood</b>. Now, we do find the language of ḥazak (strength) in commandments, as the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua (Joshua 1:7): <b>Only be strong and very courageous, to observe to do according to all the Torah that Moses My servant commanded you</b>, and so Joshua said to Israel (Joshua 23:6): <b>And you shall be very courageous to observe and to do all that is written in the book of the Torah of Moses</b> — and this is concerning all the commandments, where <b>to observe</b> alludes to negative commandments and <b>to do</b> alludes to positive commandments. But regarding a single commandment, this is not found, and what need is there for strengthening in a mere abstention from a single negative commandment? Rather, it seems to me that He mentioned strengthening in it because of the matter through which they had become attached to blood in Egypt, for they were constantly sacrificing their sacrifices to the goat-demons, as it is written (Leviticus 17:7): \"and they shall no longer sacrifice their sacrifices to the goat-demons after whom they go astray,\" and it is written (Deuteronomy 32:17): \"they sacrificed to demons, not Elohim.\" And that worship consisted of eating from the blood, for they would gather the blood for the demons and eat upon it and from it, as though they were invited by the demons to eat at the table of those demons and to commune with them. This has already been mentioned in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:46). And this is not that this is the primary reason for the prohibition of blood, for Scripture explains its reason: \"for the blood is the life,\" as in the portion of Acharei Mot (Leviticus 17:11) — but because of this, they were immersed in it and pursued it exceedingly. And behold, they would prophesy by means of it and foretell the future. Therefore Scripture came and warned that if one who eats blood tells him something future, and the sign or wonder comes to pass, let not his heart be persuaded, but let him hold fast to his integrity and his faith in HaShem, and let him not eat blood in any manner whatsoever, and let him not conceal that practice. From their words he shall not fear, and because of them he shall not be dismayed, for they are vanity, the work of delusion. He warns here in the same manner as the warning given concerning a false prophet, because of his deceptions."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The word נחירה refers to a method of slaughter where the trachea and esophagus are severed from within the neck, causing the blood to remain in the meat rather than being drained."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:49:28.990979178Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}